<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Niagara Escarpment</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Niagara Escarpment is a long ridge of resistant dolomitic limestone running from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> State through southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> and around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niagara-river">Niagara River</a> plunges over it at Niagara Falls. Its outcrops provided chert for the stone tools of indigenous peoples such as the Neutral, and the Ontario section is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> biosphere reserve.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/niagara-escarpment.jpg" alt="Niagara Escarpment" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Niagara_Escarpment_map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
